---
import Base from '../layouts/Base.astro';
import DisassemblyHero from '../components/DisassemblyHero.astro';
import OtpDemo from '../components/OtpDemo.astro';
import CaseCards from '../components/CaseCards.astro';
import QuickInstall from '../components/QuickInstall.astro';
import FieldNotes from '../components/FieldNotes.astro';
import {tutorials} from '../data/tutorials';
---
<Base>
  <section class="cover" aria-labelledby="hero-title"><div class="cover-grid" aria-hidden="true"></div><DisassemblyHero/><div class="cover-copy wrap"><p class="eyebrow"><span class="blue-dot"></span> THE REA TOOL / INDEPENDENT PRACTICAL GUIDES</p><h1 id="hero-title" class="rea-heading"><span class="hero-line"><span>REA:</span></span>{' '}<span class="hero-line"><span>Reverse Engineer Anything</span></span></h1><p class="hero-summary">Understand how software works. Rebuild the feature you need.<br/>Learn REA with guided investigations, interactive examples and tested code.</p><div class="intro-actions"><a class="button primary" href="#quick-install">Install REA with AI <span>↓</span></a><a class="button secondary" href="/tutorials/">Explore REA tutorials <span>↗</span></a></div><p class="client-note">WITH CLAUDE CODE · CODEX · CURSOR</p></div><div class="cover-bottom wrap"><span>INDEPENDENT REA LEARNING RESOURCE</span><a href="#play-cases">PICK SOMETHING TO TAKE APART <span>↓</span></a></div></section>
  <QuickInstall/>
  <section class="rea-overview wrap" id="what-is-rea" aria-labelledby="rea-definition"><div><p class="eyebrow">THE TOOL BEHIND THE INVESTIGATIONS</p><h2 id="rea-definition">What is REA?</h2></div><div><p><strong>REA (Reverse Engineer Anything)</strong> connects your coding agent to tools for investigating apps, binaries and websites. Your agent calls REA through MCP to inspect code and trace a feature, then uses the evidence to explain it or build and test a version for your project.</p><p>Start with one question: where does an export go, how is a login code calculated, or why does a game speed up? These independent guides help you follow the evidence and check the result. Recovering one rule does not establish that a whole app has been rebuilt.</p><div class="overview-links"><a href="/install/">Install REA →</a><a href="/practice/">How people use REA →</a><a href="/compare/">Compare REA →</a><a href="/first-analysis/">How to use REA →</a><a href="https://github.com/morluto/rea">Official REA project ↗</a></div></div></section>
  <FieldNotes/>
  <CaseCards/>
  <section class="investigations wrap" id="investigations"><div class="section-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">START WITH A FEATURE YOU WANT TO UNDERSTAND</p><h2>Small questions. Real discoveries.</h2></div><a class="text-link" href="/tutorials/">All 13 guides <span>↗</span></a></div><div class="feature-grid">
    <a class="feature-card" href="/cases/aegis/"><div class="card-art code-art" aria-hidden="true"><span class="art-label">59s → 60s</span><span class="digits">287<span>082</span></span><span class="digits next-digits">359<span>152</span></span><div class="tick-line"></div></div><div class="feature-copy"><span class="lesson-kind">ANDROID · WORKED CASE + LIVE DEMO</span><h3>Rebuild an authenticator’s<br/>30-second code.</h3><p>Find the rule inside an APK. Move time forward. Watch the code change.</p><span class="lesson-action">Open the Aegis walkthrough <b>↗</b></span></div></a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/tutorials/electron-clipboard/"><div class="card-art clipboard-art" aria-hidden="true"><div class="copy-sheet"><span>Project notes</span><strong>Keep the <b>good ideas.</b></strong><small>text/plain + text/html</small></div><span class="copy-hop">COPY <i>→</i> IPC <i>→</i> CLIPBOARD</span></div><div class="feature-copy"><span class="lesson-kind">ELECTRON · INTERMEDIATE</span><h3>How does Notion<br/>copy rich text?</h3><p>Trace one copy action from the editor, across processes, into the clipboard.</p><span class="lesson-action">Follow the clipboard path <b>↗</b></span></div></a>
    <a class="feature-card" href="/tutorials/native-error/"><div class="card-art native-art" aria-hidden="true"><span class="art-label">FOLLOW THE BRANCH</span><div><span>size &gt; limit</span><i>↓</i><strong>File too large.</strong><span class="address">STRING → XREF → FUNCTION</span></div></div><div class="feature-copy"><span class="lesson-kind">NATIVE · ADVANCED WORKFLOW</span><h3>What really triggers<br/>“File too large”?</h3><p>Turn an error message into a function, a condition, and a boundary test.</p><span class="lesson-action">Investigate the size check <b>↗</b></span></div></a>
  </div></section>
  <section class="example-section wrap" aria-labelledby="example-title"><div class="example-copy"><p class="eyebrow">TRY THE RESULT BEFORE YOU READ THE GUIDE</p><h2 id="example-title">One second later.<br/><span class="muted">A different code.</span></h2><p>An authenticator turns time into a counter, then a six-digit code. Drag the clock across 60 seconds and see the rule for yourself.</p><ol class="plain-steps"><li><strong>Locate the method in the APK.</strong><span>Follow the timestamp input through its calls.</span></li><li><strong>Rebuild just the calculation.</strong><span>Counter → HMAC → six digits.</span></li><li><strong>Test it against known answers.</strong><span>Check time boundaries and leading zeroes.</span></li></ol><a class="text-link" href="/cases/aegis/">Read the full investigation <span>→</span></a><p class="example-note">Based on the official Aegis investigation. This independently written demo uses public test data; the guide explains exactly what was verified.</p></div><OtpDemo/></section>
  <section class="more-guides wrap"><div class="section-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">GO BEYOND THE FIRST PROMPT</p><h2>Bring a question. Leave with a method.</h2></div></div><div class="editorial-list">{tutorials.filter(t=>!['electron-clipboard','native-error'].includes(t.slug)).map((t,i)=><a href={`/tutorials/${t.slug}/`}><span class="row-number">0{i+4}</span><div><span class="lesson-kind">{t.category.toUpperCase()} · {t.minutes.toUpperCase()}</span><h3>{t.title}</h3><p>{t.description}</p></div><span class="row-arrow">↗</span></a>)}</div></section>
  <section class="learning-path wrap"><div class="section-top"><div><p class="eyebrow">NEW TO REA?</p><h2>Start here. Then go deeper.</h2></div><span>Three short guides to get moving.</span></div><div class="lesson-links"><a href="/install/" class="lesson-link"><span class="lesson-index">01</span><div><span class="lesson-kind">SETUP · 6 MIN</span><h3>Connect your coding agent</h3><p>Install REA, choose your client and check the connection.</p><span class="lesson-action">Installation guide <b>→</b></span></div></a><a href="/first-analysis/" class="lesson-link"><span class="lesson-index">02</span><div><span class="lesson-kind">BEGINNER · 8 MIN</span><h3>Find an app’s export folder</h3><p>Trace a sample app’s button through its bridge to a CSV file.</p><span class="lesson-action">Your first investigation <b>→</b></span></div></a><a href="/cases/slugify/" class="lesson-link"><span class="lesson-index">03</span><div><span class="lesson-kind">TESTED PACKAGE · 7 MIN</span><h3>Why is “!” still in my URL?</h3><p>Read the actual package code and verify the option that fixes it.</p><span class="lesson-action">A small worked example <b>→</b></span></div></a></div></section>
  <section class="start-banner wrap"><div><h2>Your agent. Better questions.</h2><p>REA supplies the tools. These guides help you decide what to investigate next.</p></div><a class="button primary" href="/tutorials/">Find your next tutorial <span>↗</span></a></section>
</Base>
